==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|smerian}}, {{m|ang|smierwan||to anoint or rub with grease, oil, etc.}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*smerwją}}. Compare {{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|schmieren}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|smî}}, {{IPA|/smɪə/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|smîr}}, {{IPA|/smiɚ/|lang=en}}, {{IPA|/smɪɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪə(r)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-uk-smear.ogg|Audio (UK)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To spread (a substance, especially one that colours or is dirty) across a surface by rubbing.
#: ''The artist '''smeared''' paint over the canvas in broad strokes.''
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To have a substance smeared on (a surface).
#: ''She '''smeared''' her lips with lipstick.''
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To damage someone's reputation by slandering, misrepresenting, or otherwise making false accusations about an individual, their statements, or their actions.
#: ''The opposition party attempted to '''smear''' the candidate by spreading incorrect and unverifiable rumors about their personal behavior.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To become spread by smearing.
#: ''The paint is still wet &mdash; don't touch it or it will '''smear'''.''
# {{cx|climbing|lang=en}} To climb without using [[foothold]]s, using the friction from the shoe to stay on the wall.

